\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.9--85.9\% four-class; d1--d3: 58.9--81.5\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods across all 9 cells.
Four classifiers (RF, XGBoost, ordinal MLP, prototypical) converge within 0.8\,pp under cross-model transfer (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}), confirming a feature-determined accuracy ceiling.

\paragraph{Neural end-to-end baselines.}
DeBERTa-v3-base (LoRA) underperforms RF by 16--32\,pp (d2 recall 8.4\%); RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal contrastive; 64.5\%) narrows the gap but remains 14\,pp below RF.

\paragraph{Binary detectors and membership inference.}
Perturbation-based (DetectGPT, 28.8\%) and membership-inference (Min-K\% Prob, 33.2\%; Min-K\%++ \cite{zhang2024_minkpp} comparable) methods produce scores with no ordinal resolution (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}); CORAL calibration does not close the gap (d1--d3: 40.4\% vs.\ RF 71.6\%).
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024}, calibrated with 11-feature RF, independently converges to 78.1\% ($p{>}0.05$ vs.\ our 78.4\%); merging all 30 features yields 82.6\% (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_ablation_bino}).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); zero-shot LLMs (best 36.2\%; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}) also fail; depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Method comparison (RF mean across 9 cells, 5-fold CV). Chance = 25\% / 33.3\% (d1--d3). $^\dagger$Adapted from binary detection; Binoculars 11-feat: $p{>}0.05$ vs.\ 19-feat RF (paired permutation test).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Calibration & 4-Cl (\%) & d1--d3 (\%) \\
\midrule
RF 19-feature (ours) & RF & 78.4 & \textbf{71.6} \\
Binoculars$^\dagger$ \cite{binoculars2024} & RF (11 feat) & 78.1 & --- \\
Merged (30 feat)$^\dagger$ & RF & \textbf{82.6} & --- \\
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024} & threshold & 69.8 & --- \\
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024} & RF (1 feat) & 63.7 & --- \\
RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal) & contrastive & 64.5 & --- \\
DeBERTa-v3 (end-to-end) & cross-entropy & 53.1--60.6 & --- \\
\midrule
Zero-shot LLM (best) & --- & 36.2 & --- \\
Min-K\% Prob \cite{shi2024_minkprob} & CORAL & 33.2 & 40.4 \\
DetectGPT \cite{mitchell2023_detectgpt} & best-of-3 & 28.8 & 24.3 \\
Random & --- & 25.0 & 33.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The confusion matrices (Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix}) reveal that d0 is nearly perfectly separable, while most classification errors concentrate on the d2/d3 boundary and propagate to d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices: best in-distribution (GPT-2 XL temp, 85.9\%) and best LOO transfer (OLMo, 69.2\%). d0 recall ${>}$99\%; errors spread to d1--d3 under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} reports leave-one-model-family-out evaluation (nucleus only).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 mean 55.4\%; QWK\,=\,0.81; 94.8\% errors at $|\Delta| \leq 1$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (d1--d3: 55.4\%, $2.7{\times}$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline; d2 worst case 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy} reports leave-one-strategy-out transfer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Cross-strategy transfer reveals a structural barrier: d1--d3 balanced accuracy drops to 45.9\% (four-class: 59.1\%), 7\,pp below cross-model transfer (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).
Decoding strategies induce qualitatively distinct degradation trajectories; a two-stage pipeline recovers 11.5\,pp but presupposes known strategy (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).
Our depth estimation claims hold most strongly within-strategy or when the decoding configuration is known.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations (14B)}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

At 14B (Qwen2.5-14B), self-reference yields 50.8\% ($2{\times}$ chance, $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$); cross-reference control (Pythia-1.4B) drops accuracy to 44.1\% ($-$6.8\,pp) but raises $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}$ to 2 for nucleus and temperature, revealing that ${\sim}$7\,pp of the accuracy drop is attributable to reference-model mismatch rather than scale (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
Cross-domain (arXiv) accuracy reaches 74.1\% at 1--1.5B; cross-domain FPR is discussed in Limitations.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Depth signal concentrates in perplexity tails because recursive training shifts the bulk distribution only gradually; erosion appears first in upper quantiles \cite{dohmatob2024_tale_of_tails}.
The strategy effect (9.3\,pp) exceeding the model effect (7.2\,pp) implies auditing pipelines must account for decoding configuration.
Full-replacement chains provide the discriminability ceiling (d1--d3: 59--82\%); pilot accumulation ($-$14 to $-$15\,pp) and 14B cross-reference ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}$: 0${\to}$2, ${\sim}$7\,pp reference-model mismatch) confirm signal survives but attenuates with mixing and scale.

These findings motivate depth-aware curation (retaining shallow synthetic data while filtering deeply recursive passages; \citealp{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse}) and highlight open directions including non-monotonic 7--8B AUC profiles (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells at the 1--1.5B parameter scale, evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 68.9\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.9\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature) four-class (d1--d3 balanced: 58.9--81.5\%), all exceeding the 25\%/33\% chance baselines.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (75.8--85.9\%), followed by OLMo (74.4--82.3\%) and Pythia (68.9--78.7\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 9.3~pp, comparable to the 7.2~pp mean within-strategy model gap; decoding strategy contributes at least as much as model family to performance variance.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Four-class RF accuracy (\%) with bootstrap 95\% CI (${\pm}$half-width; $B{=}1{,}000$). RF with 200 trees, evaluated via 5-fold stratified CV (seed=42). Parenthesized: d1--d3 balanced accuracy from the same 4-class RF (chance = 25\% / 33\%). Depth-0 recall exceeds 97\% in all cells. Within-distribution ordinal quality: mean absolute error (MAE)\,=\,0.23, QWK\,=\,0.89 (QWK$_{\text{d1--d3}}$\,=\,0.74); 98.2\% of errors fall on adjacent depths ($|\Delta| \leq 1$). $^\dagger$Self-reference scoring (Pythia-1.4B is both generator and reference model); 4--11\,pp advantage over cross-model references (Appendix~\ref{app:ref_ablation}). Cross-cell comparisons involving Pythia should be interpreted with this caveat.}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
& Nucleus & Temp.\ $\tau{=}0.9$ & Top-$k$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B$^\dagger$  & 78.7{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(71.8) & 76.8{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(69.3) & 68.9{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(58.9) \\
OLMo-1B     & 78.9{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(72.7) & 82.3{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(76.6) & 74.4{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(66.2) \\
GPT-2~XL    & \textbf{84.3}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.5}\,(79.6) & \textbf{85.9}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.4}\,(81.5) & \textbf{75.8}{\scriptsize$\pm$0.6}\,(68.1) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-class recall.}
Depth-0 recall exceeds 97\% in all 9 cells.
Among generated depths, d2 consistently yields the lowest recall (44--73\%), followed by d3 (61--80\%) and d1 (72--91\%).
The d2 trough reflects its position at the center of the depth gradient, where features overlap with both d1 and d3, consistent with d2-vs-d3 as the EDD bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).
Full per-class recall appears in Appendix~\ref{app:per_class}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Consistency Across the Grid}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Multicollinearity is substantial (VIF${>}$10 for 14--16 of 19 features); importance rankings below indicate feature \emph{family} contributions rather than individual effects; permutation importance (Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}) partially mitigates collinearity bias \cite{strobl2008_conditional,hooker2021_permutation}.
Despite the 17.1~pp accuracy spread across cells, the perplexity tail statistics family (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{p75\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) and surprisal variance (\texttt{var\_surprisal}) dominate depth discrimination in every setting.
Within this family, \texttt{p90\_ppl} and \texttt{var\_surprisal} are consistently prominent, though individual rankings should be interpreted cautiously given VIF${>}$10; group-level ablation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:features}, below) provides the collinearity-robust confirmation.\footnote{The group-level dominance of perplexity tail statistics is robust across importance measures (Gini, SHAP, permutation; Kendall's $W{=}0.90$, $p{<}0.005$).}
Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance} shows the full per-cell ranking.

Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$ (range $[0.619, 0.954]$): models agree on which features carry depth information even when they differ in absolute accuracy.
Among strategies, top-$k$ yields the highest cross-model rank correlation ($\rho = 0.811$): the deterministic vocabulary truncation imposes a more uniform feature landscape across architectures, at the cost of weaker overall signal (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).

Feature ablation confirms this concentration: the top-5 features achieve 97.6\% of full accuracy (76.5\% vs.\ 78.4\%); group-level ablation shows perplexity features alone reach 76.6\%, surprisal 73.2\%, lexical diversity ${\leq}$47\%, and repetition ${\leq}$40\%.\footnote{Best single feature (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) achieves 58.3\%, providing a more competitive single-feature baseline than mean perplexity (25.1\%).}

The feature hierarchy shifts with depth.
At shallow boundaries (d1 vs.\ d2), \texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates importance; at deeper boundaries (d3 vs.\ d4, d4 vs.\ d5), tail perplexity statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) take over.
This transition aligns with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursion narrows the surprisal distribution (captured by its variance), while later steps erode the perplexity tail (captured by upper quantiles and kurtosis).

